    "q-161445": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161445",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y<1-x\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\nII. $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\nIII. $\\text{int}\\,A=A$",
      "worked_solution": "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14134863/assets/5151df3c5b84ffaca9aa16b401cd9364fea10a240d39c9c28d815b36e417b16d.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
